  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Salient Crgt reported $1.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 63 awards with an average award size of $20.2M. Salient Crgt increased obligations up 55.9% year over year, following a multi-year rise from $240M in FY21, $405M in FY22, $505M in FY23, $557M in FY24, and $816M in FY25. Salient Crgt concentrated most FY2026 spending in Computer Systems Design Services, which accounted for $1.1B of obligations. Salient Crgt received the largest shares from the General Services Administration and the Department of Veterans Affairs, which obligated $718M and $274M respectively. Salient Crgt also recorded significant obligations from the Department of Homeland Security and the Department of Housing and Urban Development, which obligated $121M and $65.4M respectively. Salient Crgt recorded smaller FY2026 obligations from the Department of Defense and several independent agencies, including the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation and Commodity Futures Trading Commission. This profile shows Salient Crgt anchored in IT services work with heavy reliance on a few large buying agencies and a limited set of other NAICS markets in the current fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the FY2026 obligations growth?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services drove the FY2026 obligations concentration, with $1.1B in obligations. The General Services Administration obligated $718M and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $274M in FY2026 to date, supporting the increase. Other agencies added to the total, including the Department of Homeland Security with $121M and the Department of Housing and Urban Development with $65.4M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Salient Crgt revenues by agency and market?",
        "body": "Salient Crgt shows high agency concentration: the General Services Administration obligated $718M, more than any other buyer. Salient Crgt shows high market concentration in Computer Systems Design Services with $1.1B, while the next largest NAICS is Engineering Services at $67M. Other NAICS markets and smaller agencies together account for the remaining obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
